/-!
# The dimension form of archimedean local Tate duality at an involution

For an involution `c` (`c ^ 2 = 1`) of `G_k = Ω ≃ₐ[k] Ω` and a finite `𝔽_p[Gal(K/k)]`-module `M`,
the two local groups `H¹(⟨c⟩, M)` and `H¹(⟨c⟩, M^∨(1))` (with `M^∨(1) = DualMod p (M ⊗ T)`)
have the same dimension.  This is the numerical form of the archimedean (real place) local Tate
duality, and it is the local input of the Poitou–Tate assembly.

The proof is a direct computation.

* For odd `p`, `2` is invertible in `𝔽_p`, so every `1`-cocycle on the group `⟨c⟩` of order two is a
  coboundary; both sides vanish.

* For `p = 2`, the coefficient module `T` is `⟨c⟩`-trivial, and a locally constant `1`-cocycle on
  `⟨c⟩` is determined by its value at `c`.  Writing `S_X = (addSelf _ c : X →ₗ X)`,
  `x ↦ x + c·x` (which satisfies `S_X² = 0` in characteristic two), the assignment `f ↦ f c` gives
  a linear equivalence `H¹(⟨c⟩, X) ≃ ker S_X ⧸ range S_X`, whence

    `finrank H¹(⟨c⟩, X) = finrank X - 2 * finrank (range S_X)`.

  For `X = M` this is one side.  For `X = DualMod p (M ⊗ T)`, the map `S_X` is the transpose of
  `S_M` (`T` being trivial and of rank one, `M ⊗ T ≃ M`), so its rank is the rank of `S_M`, and
  `finrank (DualMod p (M ⊗ T)) = finrank M`; the two dimensions agree.
-/

